Then the unclean spirit goes out and takes with it seven other spirits more wicked than itself. They go and live there. That man is in a worse state than before. This is how it will be with the wicked people of today." Jesus' mother and brothers
While Jesus was still speaking to the crowd, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to speak to him.
They said to him, "Your mother and your brothers are standing outside. They want to speak to you."
Jesus replied to him, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus, describing his disciples, said: "Behold my mother! Behold my brethren!"
I have no brother or sister or mother, except the one who does the will of my Father in heaven."
The Farmer's Story
On the same day, Jesus left the house and sat down by the Sea of Galilee.
A large crowd gathered around him. So he got into a boat. He sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then He spoke many things to them in parables. He said, "A certain man went out to sow his seed.
He scattered the seeds on the ground. Some of them fell on the road. The birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell on rocky places, where there was not much soil. The plants sprouted quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When the sun came out, the plants burned. They dried up because they had no roots.
The last seeds fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and choked the plants.
And another seed has fallen on fertile ground. And it yielded a harvest 100, 60 or 30 times greater than what was sown.
"Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear."
The students came to him. They asked him, "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
Answer, "You have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. It was not given to outsiders."
Anyone who has this kind of knowledge will get more. In fact, they will get a lot. If no one has this kind of knowledge, even the little they have will be taken away from them.
This is how I use parables when I speak to people. I say, "They look, but they do not really see. They listen, but they do not really hear or understand.
In them the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled. He said, " 'You will hear but not understand. You will see but not perceive. For the hearts of these people are hardened, and their ears are dulled, and they have closed their eyes, so that they might not see with their eyes, nor hear with their ears, nor understand with their hearts, and to turn and to heal them.'"
The hearts of these people have become callous; they barely hear with their ears, they have closed their eyes, otherwise they might see with their eyes, hear with their ears, understand with their hearts, and turn to the Lord, and then he would heal them. (Isaiah 6:9,10)
But blessed are your eyes because they see. And blessed are your ears because they hear.
What's there is that I want to tell you it's real. Many prophets and righteous people wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Gather! Here is the story of the farmers.
People hear the message about the Kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the devil comes. He steals what was sown in their hearts. These people are like seeds sown along the road.
Others received the seed that fell on rocky places. They are the ones who hear the message and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no roots. Therefore, they last for only a little while. They quickly abandon their faith when trouble or suffering comes because of the message.
Others received the seed that fell among the thorns. They are the ones who hear the message. But then the anxieties of life and the false promises of wealth choke them. They will prevent them from producing fruit.
But others received the seeds that fell on good soil. They are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce a crop 100, 60, or 30 times more than the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. "Thus is the kingdom of heaven," he said. "A man sowed good seed in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat has started to grow and form a grain. At the same time, weeds have appeared.
The master's slaves came to him. They said, "Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Then where have these weeds come from?"
He said, "An enemy did this." The slaves asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"No," the owner replied. "When you pull out the weeds, you might pull out the wheat along with it."
Allow both to grow together until harvest. At that time I will tell the workers what to do. Here is what I will tell them. First gather the weeds. Bind them in bundles to be burned. Then gather the wheat. Bring it to my barn." "Stories of the Mustard Seed and the Yeast"
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. He said, "The Kingdom of Heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took the seed and planted it in a field.
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it is the largest plant in the garden, all. It becomes a tree. The birds come and rest in its branches."
Jesus told them another parable. "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast," he said. "A woman mixed it into a large amount of flour. The yeast worked its way through the entire dough."
Jesus said all these things to the crowds in parables. He did not say anything to them without a parable.
So the words of the Prophet that he spoke became true. He said, "I will open my mouth and tell stories. I will speak about the things that were hidden since the creation of the world." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus explains the parable of the mustard seed.
Then Jesus left the crowds and went into the house. His disciples came to him and said, "Explain to us the parable of the weed in the field."
Answer, "The one who sowed the good seed is the Son of Man.
The world is the field. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. The harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The mint is being pulled and burned in the fire. So it will be on the Day of Reckoning.
The human child will send his angels. They will bring out from his kingdom everything that causes sin. They will also bring out everyone who does evil.
They throw them into the blazing furnace. There people will weep and grind their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in their father's kingdom. Those who have ears must listen. Stories of hidden treasure and pearls.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again, went away, and sold everything he had and bought that field.
And Ra's, Queen of the Sky, wipes for the sea and the land that stretches.
He found one that was very old. So he went and sold all that he had. And he bought that pearl. The story of the net.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the lake. All kinds of fish fell into the net.
When it was full, the fishermen drew it ashore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they cast the bad fish away.
This is how it will be on the day of reckoning. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
They throw the evil people into the blazing furnace. There, the evil ones will cry and gnash their teeth.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes," they answered.
He said to them: "Every scribe who is a trained teacher of the law of the kingdom of heaven is like the master of a household. He brings out from his treasure what is new and what is old." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories. Then he moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began to teach the people in their synagogue. They were amazed. "Where did this man get this wisdom? Where did he get this power to do miracles?" they asked.
"Is not this the son of the carpenter? Is not his mother's name Mary? Are not his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?"
Not all of his sisters are with us, so where did this man get all these things from?"
They were not at all satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country, and among his own kin. And he said to them, 'You will surely not give me any of your food to eat.' And they began to test him, seeking from him a sign from heaven."
When they had no faith, there were few miracles performed there.
John the Baptist's head is being cut off.
At that time, Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
During the reign of Herod, John had been arrested. He had been bound and placed in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod Philip's brother.
John was saying to Herod, "It is against the law for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod very much.
So he swore an oath that he would give her anything she asked for.
Her mother said to her, "What are you going to say?" The girl said to Herod, "I want the head of John the Baptist on a large platter."
The king was very angry. But he thought of his promise and his guests for dinner. So he told one of his men to give her what she asked for.
During the reign of Herod, John was beheaded in prison.
His head came in a large dish, and his to the girl. After that she carried him to her mother.
The disciples of John faded away and came to take his body and bury him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The people heard about this. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. He healed their sick.
When evening came, the disciples went to him and said, "This is a deserted place, and it is already late. Send the crowds away, so they can go and buy food in the villages."
Jesus replied, "There is no need for them to go. You give them something to eat."
They told us, "We have nothing but five loaves of bread and two fish."
He said, "Bring them here to me."
Then Jesus called the people to sit down on the grass. He took five loaves and two fish. He looked up to heaven and gave thanks. He broke the loaves into pieces. Then he gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. The disciples gathered twelve baskets of leftover pieces.
About 5000 men who ate the food. Women and children also ate food. Jesus walks on water.
Immediately, Jesus made the disciples get into the boat. He made them go before him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then he sent the crowd away.
After sending them away, he went up a mountain by himself to pray. When evening came, he was there alone.
The boat was far from the shore. It was being beaten by the waves because the wind was blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and were terrified. "He's a ghost!" they cried. They wept with fear.
Immediately Jesus called out to them, "Be courageous! I am. Do not be afraid."
"Lord, it is you?" Peter asked. "If it is you, tell me to come to you on the water."
"Come," Jesus said. So Peter got out of the boat. He walked on the water toward Jesus.
Well then, when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. He began to sink. He cried out, "Lord! Save me!"
Jesus stretched out his hand and grasped him. "Your faith is small!" he said. "Why did you doubt me?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind stopped.
Then those in the boat who worshipped Jesus, said, "You are truly the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and were stationed in Jenin Camp.
The men who had been living there knew Jesus. So they sent a message all through the nearby countryside. People brought all the sick to Jesus.
They wept for him, letting the sick touch the edge of his garments. And all who touched him were healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. They asked,
Why don't you hear your students when the sheikhs tell them? Your students don't wash their hands before they eat!"
Jesus replied, "Why do you not understand what the will of God is? You are eager to follow your own teachings!"
God said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) And He also said, "If anyone curses his father or his mother, he will be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to tell their parents, "Any help you have received from us is a gift intended for God."
Therefore, they do not need to honor their parents by gifting them. You render God's word useless in order to follow your own teachings.
You thought! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said,
This people afflicts me with what they say. And their hearts are further from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only human precepts." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the multitudes to Himself. He said, "Hear and understand."
What goes into your mouth does not make you 'unclean.' It is what comes out of your mouth that makes you 'unclean.'"
Then the disciples came to him. They asked, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus replied, "There are plants my Father in heaven has not planted; these will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit."
Bashar said, "Explain this to us."
"You still don't understand?" Jesus asked them.
Okay? Everything that enters your mouth goes to the stomach. After that, it goes out of the body.
But it's the things that come out of the mouth that actually come from the heart. It's what makes you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. Also theft, false witness, and lying about others.
These are the things that make you "unclean." However, eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A Canaanite woman who lived near Tyre and Sidon came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! A demon is oppressing my daughter. She is suffering greatly."
Jesus didn't say a word. His disciples came to him. They pleaded, "Send her away. She's still screaming after us."
Jesus answered, "I was only sent to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman fell on her knees in front of him. "O Lord! Help me!" she said.
Answer, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
"Yes, Lord," she said. "But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' tables."
Jesus replied, "Woman, you have great faith! What you asked for will be done." And at that moment her daughter was healed. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus left there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up on the side of a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought blind people and those who could not walk. They also brought crippled people, those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were astonished. Those who could not speak were speaking. The disabled were healed. Those who could not walk were walking. Those who were blind were seeing. Therefore, the people praised the God of Israel.
Jesus called his disciples to come to him. He said, "I am deeply concerned about these people. They have already been with me for three days. They have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do that, they will become very weak on their way home."
The disciples brought him a reply. They said, "There is nothing here." "Where can we get enough bread to feed this large crowd?"
"How many loaves do you have?" Jesus asked. "Seven," they replied, "and a few small fish."
Jesus said to the crowds, "Sit on the ground."
The seven loaves and the fish He took, and gave thanks. Then He broke them and gave them to the disciples. And the disciples passed them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. Afterward, the disciples collected seven baskets of leftover pieces.
The number of men who ate food was 4000. Women and children also ate food.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he went into the boat. He went to the region near Magadan.
People ask Jesus for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
He said: "In the evening you look at the sky. You say, "It will be beautiful. The sky is red."
And in the morning she says, 'Today there will be a storm. The sky is red and cloudy.' You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
A wicked and adulterous generation seeks for a sign, but no sign will be given to it but the sign of Jonah." And Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The students crossed to the other side of the lake. They forgot to take bread.
Jesus said to them, "Watch out, beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The students discussed this among themselves. They said, "He must say that because we didn't bring any bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. So he said, "Your faith is small! Why are you discussing among yourselves that you have no bread?"
Don't you understand yet? Don't you remember the five loaves of bread and the two fish that you gave to the 5,000? And don't you remember how many baskets full of pieces you collected?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand? Do you not remember the number of baskets you filled with fragments?
How could you not understand? Wasn't I explaining to you about the bread? But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
Then the disciples understood that Jesus had not told them to beware of the leaven of bread. He was warning them of what the Pharisees and Sadducees taught. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went about all the towns and villages, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the good news of the kingdom. There he cured many who had various diseases. And he spoke to them in parables, saying, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed, which a man took and planted in his field. It grew and became a tree, and its branches spread out, so that the birds of the air made nests in its branches."
They answered, "Some say John the Baptist; others say Elijah; and still others say Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
"To doubt me?" he said. "Who do you think I am?"
Simon Peter answered, "You are the Christ. You are the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For it is not by a man's wisdom that he possesses the kingdom of heaven;
Thus I say to you, you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church, and the gates of Hades will not be strong enough to destroy it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. Whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. Whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven."
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Christ. Jesus speaks of his coming death.
From that time Jesus began to explain to His disciples what was going to happen to Him. He told them that He must go to Jerusalem. There He must suffer at the hands of the elders, the chief priests, and the scribes. He must be killed and rise again on the third day.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "This shall never happen, Lord!" he said. "This shall never, ever happen!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. You are not thinking about the things of God. Instead, you are thinking about human things."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples. He said, "If anyone wants to follow me, he must say no to himself. He must take up his cross and follow me.
Why does he want to save his life, this this this this this this this this this this this this this this this this this this this this.
What good would it do to gain the whole world if he lost his own soul? Or what could a person give in exchange for his soul?
The son of man will come in the glory of his father with his angels, and he will reward each one according to his works.
What I want to tell you is true. Some who are standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom."
The Jass appears to have a change in appearance.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John the brother of James with him. He led them up to a high mountain. They were all alone.
Before them his appearance was transformed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became white as light.
Suddenly, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three shelter. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And while Peter was speaking, this happened. A bright cloud formed around them. A voice came from the cloud, saying, "This is my Son, whom I love. Listen to him!"
And when the disciples heard this, they were afraid. They fell on their faces.
But Jesus came and touched them. "Rise," he said. "Do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. While coming down, Jesus told them what to do. "Do not tell anyone what you have seen," he said. "Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The students asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus replied, "It is true. Elijah is supposed to come and make all things new again.
But I tell you, Elijah has come already. The people did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted to do. In the same way, they will make the Son of Man suffer."
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
The man drew near to Jesus as they approached the crowd. He knelt down before him.
"O Lord," he said, "have mercy on my son. He is trembling violently and suffering greatly. He keeps falling into the fire or into the water."
I brought him to your disciples. But they could not heal him."
"You are an evil and unfaithful people!" Jesus replied. "How long must I stay with you? How long must I put up with you? Bring the child to me."
Jesus ordered the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. He was healed at that moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and said, "Why could we not cast out the demon?"
He said, "Because your faith is very small. What I am about to tell you is true. If your faith is as small as a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there.' And it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
<.>
They met together in Galilee. Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the hands of men."
They killed him. On the third day he rose from the dead." Then the disciples were filled with great sorrow. Jesus pays the temple tax
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, tax collectors came to Peter. They asked him, "Does your teacher not pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, he does," he answered. When Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "Who is it that the kings of the earth collect taxes and tribute from? Do they collect it from their sons or from others?"
"Without," Peter answered. "In this way no one will be able to destroy it," said Jesus.
We have no desire to anger them. So go to the lake and cast your fishing line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. There you will find the exact coin you need. Take it and give it to them in payment of my tax and my tribute.
